\section*{Virasoro propagation through projective bundles}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Full descendant Virasoro constraints are claimed to pass from any smooth projective base to projectivizations of arbitrary nonsplit algebraic bundles, including all genera, integral curve classes, and odd insertions. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For a smooth connected projective complex variety $B$, an algebraic vector bundle $E$ of rank $r\ge2$, and $X=\mathbb P_B(E)$ parametrizing lines in the fibers, OpenAI claims
\[
\bigl[L_k^B Z_B=0\text{ for all }k\ge-1\bigr]\ \Longrightarrow\
\bigl[L_k^X Z_X=0\text{ for all }k\ge-1\bigr].
\]
For each target $Y$, $Z_Y$ is its ordinary descendant partition function $\exp(\sum_g\hbar^{g-1}F_g^Y)$ with all cohomology classes, stable-map cotangent classes and actual effective integral curve labels. The normally ordered $L_k^Y$ use $\mu_Y|_{H^{p,q}}=p-\dim(Y)/2$, $R_Y=c_1(TY)\cup$, and zero-mode scalar $\chi(Y)/16-\operatorname{str}(\mu_Y^2)/4$; other modes have no scalar correction. The conclusion is coefficientwise in every genus, curve class and descendant list.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 065, 2026-10-05): Localization on the completion by a trivial summand separates the base and projective-bundle blocks. The logarithm of an equivariant shift cancels coefficient differentiation in the grading, giving quantizable modes; ancestor bounds continue their identities coefficientwise, and quantum Riemann--Roch identifies the ordinary constraints on both components.

